# =============================================================================
# BUILD 01 (FAILED ATTEMPT): First-Instinct Financial Exchange in Intent
# =============================================================================
# This file captures what happens when you write a 5-collection exchange using
# natural first-instinct phrasing. Every failure below is real and instructive.
# =============================================================================

Traders is a collection.
Each trader has a handle (text), an email (text), cash_cents (number), risk_score (number) and frozen (number).
No two traders share the same handle.
No two traders share the same email.
# FAILURE 1: Replace policy with >1 unique field is rejected by check.py:138
If a trader already exists, replace it.

Assets is a collection.
Each asset has a ticker (text), mark_price (number) and volatility_bps (number).
No two assets share the same ticker.
# FAILURE 2: "Reject duplicates." worked for the 1st collection when only 1 collection
# existed, but once a 2nd collection is declared, scope() asks "which collection is this about?"
Reject duplicates.

Orders is a collection.
# FAILURE 3: Naming a foreign-key field "trader" on Orders shadows the "Traders"
# collection globally in normalize() (parser.py:434)!
Each order has an order_id (number), a trader (text), a symbol (text), a side (number), a limit_price (number) and a qty (number).
No two orders share the same order_id.
If an order already exists, reject it.

# FAILURE 4: Because "trader" is now a FIELD on Orders, any sentence about the
# "Traders" collection now fails to recognize "trader"/"traders" as a collection!
register-trader: add a trader.

# FAILURE 5: Writing "find an order by order_id" collides with the 2-word SQL phrase
# "order by" (SORTBY) in lexicon.py:90, swallowing the collection name "order"!
inspect-order: find an order by order_id.

# FAILURE 6: Mixing "and" and "or" in a condition is rejected by parser.py:689
vip-or-rich: show the traders whose frozen is 0 and risk_score is under 100 or cash_cents is at least 1000000.

# FAILURE 7: Dividing by a runtime parameter (leverage) is rejected by check.py:149
calc-liq: take entry_cents and leverage and return entry_cents - entry_cents / leverage.

# FAILURE 8: Using a taught word inside a hyphenated capability label ("check-solvent:")
# triggers a bug in expand_sentence() (parser.py:314) because '-' is a symbol token,
# causing self.expand(pre) to expand "solvent" inside the capability name before ':'!
"solvent" means frozen is 0 and risk_score under 8000.
check-solvent: show the traders whose frozen is 0.

